misc: hy310-board-mgr: hold the fan rail HIGH from the request on, and say the first RPM

Two small things for a board this project cannot put on a bench, from
analysis A14 (19.09.2026, umbau/reviews/A14.md section 5.2).

1. devm_gpio_request_one(dev, fan->power_gpio, 0, "fan-power") asks for
   flags 0, and flags 0 is GPIOF_OUT_INIT_LOW (include/linux/gpio.h in
   linux-6.18.38). The line is driven LOW by the request and only goes
   HIGH one statement later in gpio_direction_output(). On the HY310 this
   is invisible: no fan node of ours carries a fan-power-gpio, and where
   the device tree hogs PB5 the request fails with -EBUSY and the driver
   takes the "managed by hog" branch. On a board that puts the pin into
   the fan node instead of hogging it, the rail is LOW for that moment -
   and on the HY300 Pro the same PB5 also enables the LED boost, so the
   moment is a visible blink. GPIOF_OUT_INIT_HIGH, which is what the
   usb-power request in the same driver already uses, removes it. The
   driver's own comment already says the intent is HIGH.

2. One dev_info with the first RPM reading the driver trusts (the first
   poll after system_state reaches SYSTEM_RUNNING - before that the value
   is not judged either). An owner who tests a board for us then has the
   proof that the tacho is wired and the fan turns in the journal of a
   normal boot, without a shell session and without asking him to read
   sysfs. It is one line per boot and per fan; nothing else in the
   polling, the warn threshold or fan_stall_shutdown changes.

Tested on the HY310: no behaviour change. Its fan node has no
fan-power-gpio, so hunk 3 cannot fire at all there, and the new line is
one dev_info in the boot log next to the "fan0: ... RPM below ..." the
driver could already print.

diff -ruN a/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c b/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c
--- a/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c
@@ -178,6 +178,7 @@
  * @rpm_warn_speed:  RPM below which a warning cycle is counted
  * @rpm_warn_cnt:    Threshold of consecutive warn cycles → power off
  * @rpm_warn_cycles: Current consecutive warn cycle count
+ * @boot_rpm_logged: True once the first reading after boot has been logged
  * @valid:           True if this fan slot is populated
  */
 struct hy310_fan {
@@ -196,6 +197,7 @@
 	u32			 rpm_warn_speed;
 	u32			 rpm_warn_cnt;
 	u32			 rpm_warn_cycles;
+	bool			 boot_rpm_logged;
 	bool			 valid;
 };
 
@@ -451,6 +453,18 @@
 			continue;
 
 		/*
+		 * One line per boot with the first reading the driver
+		 * trusts. On a board nobody here can put on a bench, its
+		 * owner's journal is the only proof that the tacho is wired
+		 * and that the fan turns; after this line the driver is as
+		 * quiet as before.
+		 */
+		if (!fan->boot_rpm_logged) {
+			fan->boot_rpm_logged = true;
+			dev_info(priv->dev, "fan%d: %u RPM at boot\n", i, rpm);
+		}
+
+		/*
 		 * A stall is the one condition this board can detect and must
 		 * act on: the fan is an unregulated on/off load on the shared
 		 * PB5 rail, so the driver cannot restart it, and there is no
@@ -1012,8 +1026,15 @@
 		 * Set fan power HIGH. Use gpio_request + gpio_direction_output
 		 * explicitly to ensure the pin is driven HIGH even after
 		 * rmmod/insmod cycles when the DTS gpio-hog is no longer active.
+		 *
+		 * The request itself already drives the pin HIGH. Flags 0 is
+		 * GPIOF_OUT_INIT_LOW (include/linux/gpio.h), which would put
